    "candidate_text": "For each \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), we have \\(x^n\\to0\\), while \\(f_n(1)=1\\) for every \\(n\\). Thus the pointwise limit is\n\\[\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}0,&0\\le x<1,\\\\1,&x=1.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nAny uniform limit would necessarily equal this pointwise limit. Fix \\(\\varepsilon=\\tfrac12\\). For every \\(N\\in\\mathbb Z_+\\), let \\(n=N\\) and \\(x=2^{-1/N}\\). Then \\(x<1\\), so \\(f(x)=0\\), whereas\n\\[\n|f_n(x)-f(x)|=x^N=\\frac12.\n\\]\nHence for every \\(N\\) there exist \\(n\\ge N\\) and \\(x\\in[0,1]\\) such that \\(|f_n(x)-f(x)|\\ge\\tfrac12\\). Therefore \\((f_n)\\) does not converge uniformly on \\([0,1]\\).",
